-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CRYPTOGRAPHY
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This software implements no cryptographic algorithm of its own and contains no
homegrown or proprietary cryptographic construction. Its own cryptography is
performed entirely through standard platform primitives: Node.js `node:crypto`,
WebCrypto at the browser edge, and the Windows Data Protection API (DPAPI) for
local credential storage.

One third-party library in this tree performs cryptography, and it is named
here rather than omitted. `werift` (MIT licensed, version pinned exactly) is a
pure-JavaScript implementation of WebRTC. It is loaded lazily by
`src/lib/online-fra-direct-transport.js` alone, to open a peer-to-peer data
channel between a person's own two computers, and WebRTC secures that channel
with DTLS -- so the DTLS handshake and record protection on that one path are
werift's, not this software's. It is optional at runtime: when it is absent the
transport reports the direct path unavailable and traffic stays on the relay
path, which uses the platform primitives below. `playwright`, the only other
declared dependency, performs no cryptography.

The primitives this software itself calls are:

  - X25519 key agreement, for establishing session keys between a person's
    own computers.
  - AES-256-GCM authenticated encryption, for the session frames themselves
    and for data sealed at rest.
  - Ed25519 signatures, for device identity, signed leases, and offline
    licence verification.
  - SHA-256, for content addressing, integrity checks and audit chaining.
  - HKDF and PBKDF2, for key derivation.

Two non-cryptographic uses of hash functions appear in the source and are
named here so that an inventory of this tree does not read as though they
were security mechanisms. Neither protects anything:

  - MD5 in `src/lib/search.js`, as the bucket-selection function of a
    feature-hashed lexical embedding vector. It maps a token to an index in
    a fixed-width array. It is not used to authenticate, sign, protect or
    compare anything.
  - SHA-1 in `packages/internal-vcs/`, where it identifies which object
    format a git repository uses. That is git's own on-disk format, read
    rather than chosen by this software.

